    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "Sure. Well, the bourbon boom has been a godsend to Kentucky in so many ways. First of all, it's another part of Kentucky's identity. It's become, I mean, it always has been to a certain extent, but not to the extent it is now. Well, now I'm rarely on a plane coming back to Louisville where there's not one or more groups on the plane who are coming down to the Bourbon Trail, either the Urban Bourbon Trail or the major Distilleries Bourbon Trail. So it's become part of our culture and our brand and in a very popular brand. Economically, I think when I formed the Bourbon Caucus, it was about a two and a half to three billion dollar industry in Kentucky and it's now somewhere over eight billion dollars. with the commensurate increases in employment. So it's been a huge contributor to directly to the economy. But then you add the tourism element and the numbers are truly astounding. We opened a wonderful hotel a year and a half ago and we'll be on the hotel. And I was touring with the manager, and he said, you know, we were projecting, this was in March of 2019, we were projecting for the rest of the year, 52, 53% occupancy, and we're already at 75% occupancy. And I asked him, well, how much of that increase is attributable to verminism or",
